    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "In triangle $VWX$, we have $\\triangle VWX$ where $VX=\\sqrt{13}$ and $VW=3$. To find $\\tan V$, we first need to find the measure of $\\angle VWX$.\n\nSince $VX$ and $VW$ are the legs of the right triangle $VWX$, we can use the Pythagorean theorem to find the length of $WX$:\n\n\\[\nWX = \\sqrt{VX^2 - VW^2} = \\sqrt{(\\sqrt{13})^2 - 3^2} = \\sqrt{13 - 9} = \\sqrt{4} = 2.\n\\]\n\nNow that we have the lengths of all three sides of the right triangle $VWX$, we can find $\\tan V$. The tangent of an angle in a right triangle is the ratio of the length of the opposite side to the length of the adjacent side. In this case, the opposite side is $WX$ and the adjacent side is $VW$:\n\n\\[\n\\tan V = \\frac{WX}{VW} = \\frac{2}{3}.\n\\]\n\nSo the final answer is $\\boxed{\\frac{2}{3}}$.",
