    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find \\(\\tan V\\) in triangle \\(VWX\\), we need to determine the length of the side \\(WX\\) and then use the definition of the tangent function in a right triangle. Here are the steps:\n\n1. **Identify the given information:**\n   - \\(VX = \\sqrt{13}\\)\n   - \\(VW = 3\\)\n   - \\(\\angle WVX = 90^\\circ\\) (since \\(VWX\\) is a right triangle)\n\n2. **Apply the Pythagorean theorem to find \\(WX\\):**\n   The Pythagorean theorem states that in a right triangle, the square of the length of the hypotenuse (\\(VX\\)) is equal to the sum of the squares of the lengths of the other two sides (\\(VW\\) and \\(WX\\)). Therefore, we can write:\n   \\[\n   VX^2 = VW^2 + WX^2\n   \\]\n   Substituting the given values:\n   \\[\n   (\\sqrt{13})^2 = 3^2 + WX^2\n   \\]\n   Simplifying this equation:\n   \\[\n   13 = 9 + WX^2\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   WX^2 = 13 - 9\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   WX^2 = 4\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   WX = \\sqrt{4} = 2\n   \\]\n\n3. **Use the definition of the tangent function:**\n   The tangent of an angle in a right triangle is the ratio of the length of the opposite side to the length of the adjacent side. For \\(\\angle V\\), the opposite side is \\(WX\\) and the adjacent side is \\(VW\\). Therefore:\n   \\[\n   \\tan V = \\frac{WX}{VW} = \\frac{2}{3}\n   \\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(\\tan V\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{2}{3}}\\).",
